<DocsLayout lang="en" id="liens" description="Put a linked copy of a chart, a range or an image in a presentation or a document, and keep it up to date.">
  <p class="prose__lead">A chart, a range of cells or an image can have copies elsewhere on the desk. The copy follows its source; a thread joins them.</p>
  <Capture lang="en" id="links" alt="Slide 3 shows the spreadsheet’s chart, marked “Linked · Chronos — tours de piste · Up to date”." />

  <h2 id="link">“Link into…”</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A chart</strong>: open the spreadsheet, select the chart, “Link into…”.</li>
    <li><strong>A range</strong>: select the cells, “Link into…”; the copy is a real table.</li>
    <li><strong>An image</strong>: select it on the desk, “Link into…”.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Then choose the presentation (new slide or last slide) or the document (at the start or the end). The copy arrives at once and a thread appears on the desk.</p>

  <h2 id="states">A thread’s states</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Thread</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Violet, dashed</td><td><strong>Up to date</strong>: the copy shows the source (“En direct” mode, the default). After a change, the copy follows within a second.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Amber</td><td><strong>Late</strong>: in “Manuel” mode, the source has changed; “Update” whenever you want.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grey, tight dashes</td><td><strong>Frozen</strong>: a snapshot taken on a date; the copy won’t change any more.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Red, broken</td><td><strong>Broken</strong>: the source was deleted; the copy keeps its last value.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Click a thread’s label (or <kbd>Tab</kbd> to it, then <kbd>Enter</kbd>): the mode, what the copy carries, “Update”, open the source or the copy, cut the link. “Desk threads” (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>L</kbd>) lists them all.</p>

  <h2 id="edit">Editing a copy</h2>
  <p>A copy keeps its own size and place. Its style changes (a restyled table, an image retouched on its own) stay on the copy and are listed in the link; “Apply to the source” carries them over to the original. Values always come from the source: to change a number, change the spreadsheet.</p>

  <h2 id="export">On export</h2>
  <p>A downloaded document or presentation holds its copies as ordinary charts, tables and images, at their last value. The link itself stays on the desk.</p>
</DocsLayout>
